{"hash":"ea66393550216677","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":89.414,"said":"Here the steps lead down to Koti Tirth Ghat, right below the temple. Watch the pilgrims: they step into the river, dip, and rise again. A bath here, it is believed, brings the merit of a crore, ten million, pilgrimages. Then they fill their vessels with Narmada water and carry it up the steps to offer to Omkareshwar. Every evening the river herself is worshipped here. The Narmada aarti is performed on this ghat, and little lamps are set floating on the current, especially at Narmada Jayanti. And on Mondays the Lord himself comes down to the water. At four in the afternoon the Somwar Sawari sets out: Omkareshwar’s golden palanquin, carrying his three-faced image, comes to this ghat for worship, goes out onto the river by boat, and returns through the town to drums and cymbals, as devotees chant ‘Om Shambhu Bholenath’. Many pilgrims also walk the parikrama of the whole island, about seven kilometres, past old temples, the meeting of two streams, and the great statue of Adi Shankaracharya on the hilltop. Now, like the pilgrims, carry the river upward. The temple is waiting at the top of the steps.\n\nDid you know? Locally, the wide, shallow channel north of the island is called the Kaveri and the narrow, deep one to the south the Narmada; Shankara’s verse sings of their meeting.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1111},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1127,"end":1294}],"words":[[0.098,0.312,0,4],[0.423,0.108,5,8],[0.543,0.433,9,14],[0.988,0.24,15,19],[1.24,0.312,20,24],[1.564,0.108,25,27],[1.685,0.397,28,32],[2.093,0.385,33,38],[2.49,0.421,39,43],[3.127,0.337,45,50],[3.475,0.276,51,56],[3.764,0.108,57,60],[3.884,0.505,61,67],[5.288,0.312,69,74],[5.613,0.096,75,78],[5.721,0.709,79,87],[6.611,0.144,89,93],[6.767,0.349,94,98],[7.127,0.192,99,103],[7.332,0.072,104,107],[7.416,0.469,108,113],[8.077,0.397,115,118],[8.678,0.24,120,123],[8.93,0.312,124,128],[9.255,0.469,129,1